1
00:00:01,879 --> 00:00:05,958
Chapter 12. Biographical.

2
00:00:05,959 --> 00:00:11,078
Quiqu was a native of Kokavoko, an island far away to the west and south.

3
00:00:11,079 --> 00:00:17,078
It is not down in any map. True places never are. When a new hatched savage,

4
00:00:17,079 --> 00:00:20,999
running wild about his native woodlands in a grass clout, followed by the

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:26,279
nibbling goats as if he were a green sapling. Even then, in Quiqu's ambitious

6
00:00:26,280 --> 00:00:30,599
soul lurked a strong desire to see something more of Christrysendom than a

7
00:00:30,600 --> 00:00:36,358
specimen whaler or two. His father was a high chief, a king, his uncle a high

8
00:00:36,359 --> 00:00:40,919
priest, and on the maternal side he boasted aunts who were the wives of

9
00:00:40,920 --> 00:00:46,039
unconquerable warriors. There was excellent blood in his veins, royal

10
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:51,317
stuff, though sadly visiated, I fear, by the cannibal propensity he nourished in

11
00:00:51,318 --> 00:00:56,599
his untuded youth. A Sag Harbor ship visited his father's bay, and Quiquake

12
00:00:56,600 --> 00:00:59,238
sought a passage to the Christian lands.

13
00:00:59,239 --> 00:01:03,958
But the ship, having her full compliment of seaman, spurned his suit, and not all

14
00:01:03,959 --> 00:01:09,477
the king his father's influence, could prevail. But Quiquake vowed a vow. Alone

15
00:01:09,478 --> 00:01:13,718
in his canoe, he paddled off to a distant straight, which he knew the ship

16
00:01:13,719 --> 00:01:18,679
must pass through when she quitted the island. On one side was a coral reef, on

17
00:01:18,680 --> 00:01:22,999
the other a low tongue of land covered with mangrove thickets that grew out

18
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,119
into the water. hiding his canoe, still afloat among these thickets, with its

19
00:01:28,120 --> 00:01:33,317
prow seawward, he sat down in the stern, paddle low in hand, and when the ship

20
00:01:33,318 --> 00:01:38,039
was gliding by, like a flash, he darted out, gained her side, and with one

21
00:01:38,040 --> 00:01:43,317
backward dash of his foot, capsized, and sank the canoe, climbed up the chains,

22
00:01:43,318 --> 00:01:47,958
and throwing himself at full length upon the deck, grappled a ringbolt there, and

23
00:01:47,959 --> 00:01:51,558
swore not to let go, though hacked in pieces.

24
00:01:51,559 --> 00:01:55,798
In vain the captain threatened to throw him overboard, suspended a cutless over

25
00:01:55,799 --> 00:02:01,798
his naked wrists. Quiqu was the son of a king, and Quiquag budged not. Struck by

26
00:02:01,799 --> 00:02:06,039
his desperate dauntlessness and his wild desire to visit Christrysendom, the

27
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:10,519
captain at last relented, and told him he might make himself at home. But this

28
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:15,958
fine young savage, this sea prince of Wales, never saw the captain's cabin.

29
00:02:15,959 --> 00:02:20,199
They put him down among the sailors and made a whaleman of him. But like Zar

30
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:25,158
Peter, content to toil in the shipyards of foreign cities, Quiqu disdained no

31
00:02:25,159 --> 00:02:29,798
seeming igniminy, if thereby he might happily gain the power of enlightening

32
00:02:29,799 --> 00:02:34,999
his untoured countrymen. For at bottom, so he told me, he was actuated by a

33
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:38,839
profound desire to learn among the Christians the arts whereby to make his

34
00:02:38,840 --> 00:02:43,078
people still happier than they were, and more than that, still better than they

35
00:02:43,079 --> 00:02:47,878
were. But alas, the practices of whalemen soon convinced him that even

36
00:02:47,879 --> 00:02:52,438
Christians could be both miserable and wicked, infinitely more so than all his

37
00:02:52,439 --> 00:02:56,839
father's heathens, arrived at last in old Sag Harbor, and seeing what the

38
00:02:56,840 --> 00:03:00,999
sailors did there, and going on to Nantucket, and seeing how they spent

39
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:06,358
their wages in that place also, poor Quiquay gave it up for lost, thought he,

40
00:03:06,359 --> 00:03:08,599
it's a wicked world in all meridians.

41
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:14,358
I'll die a pagan. And thus an old idoltor at heart, he yet lived among

42
00:03:14,359 --> 00:03:18,759
these Christians, wore their clothes, and tried to talk their gibberish, hence

43
00:03:18,760 --> 00:03:24,039
the queer ways about him, though now some time from home.

44
00:03:24,040 --> 00:03:28,279
By hence I asked him whether he did not propose going back and having a

45
00:03:28,280 --> 00:03:33,078
coronation, since he might now consider his father dead and gone, he being very

46
00:03:33,079 --> 00:03:38,679
old and feeble at the last accounts. He answered, "No, not yet," and added that

47
00:03:38,680 --> 00:03:42,839
he was fearful Christianity, or rather Christians, had unfitted him for

48
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:48,999
ascending the pure and undefiled throne of 30 pagan kings before him. But by and

49
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:54,438
by, he said he would return as soon as he felt himself baptized again. For the

50
00:03:54,439 --> 00:03:58,759
nons, however, he proposed to sail about and sew his wild oats in all four

51
00:03:58,760 --> 00:04:03,718
oceans. They had made a harpooner of him, and that barbed iron was in lie of

52
00:04:03,719 --> 00:04:08,839
a scepter. Now I asked him what might be his immediate purpose touching his

53
00:04:08,840 --> 00:04:14,119
future movements. He answered to go to sea again in his old vocation. Upon this

54
00:04:14,120 --> 00:04:17,798
I told him that whailing was my own design and informed him of my intention

55
00:04:17,799 --> 00:04:22,039
to sail out of Nantucket as being the most promising port for an adventurous

56
00:04:22,040 --> 00:04:26,839
whaleman to embark from. He had once resolved to accompany me to that island,

57
00:04:26,840 --> 00:04:31,317
ship aboard the same vessel, get into the same watch, the same boat, the same

58
00:04:31,318 --> 00:04:36,438
mess with me, in short, to share my every hap, with both my hands and his,

59
00:04:36,439 --> 00:04:41,878
boldly dip into the potluck of both worlds. To all this I joyously asented,

60
00:04:41,879 --> 00:04:45,718
for besides the affection I now felt for Quiquake, he was an experienced

61
00:04:45,719 --> 00:04:50,839
harpooner, and as such could not fail to be of great usefulness to one who, like

62
00:04:50,840 --> 00:04:55,317
me, was wholly ignorant of the mysteries of whailing, though well acquainted with

63
00:04:55,318 --> 00:04:58,759
the sea, as known to merchant seaman.

64
00:04:58,760 --> 00:05:03,878
His story being ended with his pipe's last dying puff, Quequake embraced me,

65
00:05:03,879 --> 00:05:08,039
pressed his forehead against mine, and blowing out the light, we rolled over

66
00:05:08,040 --> 00:05:14,079
from each other this way and that, and very soon were sleeping.
